    {"t": "Select the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Recieve", "Receive", "Receeve", "Recive"], "a": 1, "e": "'Receive' follows the 'i before e except after c' rule.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Definately", "Definitely", "Definitly", "Definetely"], "a": 1, "e": "The correct spelling is 'definitely'.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Accomodation", "Accommodation", "Acomodation", "Accommodattion"], "a": 1, "e": "'Accommodation' has double c and double m.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Government", "Goverment", "Governement", "Govermnent"], "a": 0, "e": "The correct spelling is 'government'.", "d": 1},
    {"t": "Select the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Embarass", "Embarrass", "Embaras", "Embarras"], "a": 1, "e": "'Embarrass' has double r and double s.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Maintainance", "Maintenance", "Maintenence", "Maintanance"], "a": 1, "e": "The correct spelling is 'maintenance'.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correctly spelt word.", "o": ["Privilege", "Priviledge", "Privelege", "Priveledge"], "a": 0, "e": "The correct spelling is 'privilege' (no 'd').", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?", "o": ["The boys' books are here.", "The boy's are here.", "The boys book's are here.", "The boy's books are here's."], "a": 0, "e": "'Boys'' shows plural possessive (books of the boys).", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.", "o": ["I need to buy eggs milk and bread.", "I need to buy eggs, milk, and bread.", "I need to buy, eggs milk and bread.", "I need, to buy eggs milk and bread."], "a": 1, "e": "Items in a list are separated by commas.", "d": 1},
    {"t": "Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon?", "o": ["He was tired; so he slept.", "He was tired; he slept.", "He; was tired he slept.", "He was tired, he; slept."], "a": 1, "e": "A semicolon joins two related independent clauses without a conjunction.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correct use of 'their', 'there' or 'they're'.", "o": ["Their going home now.", "There going home now.", "They're going home now.", "They going home now."], "a": 2, "e": "'They're' is the contraction of 'they are'.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which word completes the sentence correctly: 'It's ___ book.'", "o": ["your", "you're", "you", "yours"], "a": 0, "e": "'Your' is the possessive adjective needed before the noun 'book'.", "d": 2},

    # ── Oral forms: vowels & consonants ──
    {"t": "How many vowel sounds are there in the English language?", "o": ["5", "12", "20", "26"], "a": 2, "e": "English has 20 vowel sounds (12 monophthongs and 8 diphthongs).", "d": 2},
    {"t": "How many consonant sounds are there in English?", "o": ["20", "24", "26", "21"], "a": 1, "e": "English has 24 consonant sounds.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'ship' contains which vowel sound?", "o": ["Long /iː/", "Short /ɪ/", "Long /æ/", "Short /ʌ/"], "a": 1, "e": "'Ship' has the short vowel /ɪ/, as in 'sit'.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The vowel sound in 'seat' is a:", "o": ["Short vowel", "Long vowel", "Diphthong", "Consonant"], "a": 1, "e": "'Seat' contains the long vowel /iː/.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which of these words contains a diphthong?", "o": ["Cat", "Boy", "Bit", "Cup"], "a": 1, "e": "'Boy' contains the diphthong /ɔɪ/.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'chair' begins with which consonant sound?", "o": ["/k/", "/ʃ/", "/tʃ/", "/s/"], "a": 2, "e": "'Chair' begins with the affricate /tʃ/.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The final sound in 'sing' is:", "o": ["/g/", "/n/", "/ŋ/", "/ng/"], "a": 2, "e": "'Sing' ends with the velar nasal /ŋ/.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which word has the /θ/ (voiceless 'th') sound?", "o": ["This", "Think", "They", "Mother"], "a": 1, "e": "'Think' has the voiceless /θ/; 'this' and 'they' have voiced /ð/.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'zoo' begins with a:", "o": ["Voiceless sound", "Voiced sound", "Vowel", "Nasal"], "a": 1, "e": "'Zoo' begins with the voiced /z/.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "In 'church', the initial sound is a(n):", "o": ["Plosive", "Affricate", "Fricative", "Nasal"], "a": 1, "e": "The /tʃ/ in 'church' is an affricate.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The sound /b/ in 'bat' is classified as a:", "o": ["Fricative", "Plosive", "Nasal", "Lateral"], "a": 1, "e": "/b/ is a bilabial plosive (stop).", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The sound /s/ in 'sun' is a:", "o": ["Plosive", "Nasal", "Fricative", "Affricate"], "a": 2, "e": "/s/ is a voiceless alveolar fricative.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which word contains the /ʒ/ sound (as in 'measure')?", "o": ["Ship", "Vision", "Chair", "King"], "a": 1, "e": "'Vision' contains the voiced /ʒ/ sound.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The 'l' in 'little' is a:", "o": ["Nasal", "Lateral", "Plosive", "Vowel"], "a": 1, "e": "/l/ is a lateral consonant, made with air flowing around the sides of the tongue.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'cat' contains how many consonant sounds?", "o": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "a": 1, "e": "'Cat' has two consonants (/k/ and /t/) and one vowel.", "d": 2},

    # ── Oral forms: stress & intonation ──
    {"t": "In the word 'important', the primary stress falls on the:", "o": ["First syllable", "Second syllable", "Third syllable", "All syllables equally"], "a": 1, "e": "'imPORtant' — stress is on the second syllable.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which syllable is stressed in 'education'?", "o": ["e-", "du-", "ca-", "tion"], "a": 2, "e": "'eduCAtion' — primary stress is on the third syllable 'ca'.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'record' is stressed differently depending on whether it is used as a noun or a verb. As a NOUN, the stress is on the:", "o": ["First syllable", "Second syllable", "Both equally", "Neither"], "a": 0, "e": "Nouns take first-syllable stress (REcord); verbs take second (reCORD).", "d": 3},
    {"t": "In 'photograph', the stressed syllable is:", "o": ["PHO-", "to-", "graph", "Equal stress"], "a": 0, "e": "'PHOtograph' — stress is on the first syllable.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which word has its stress on the first syllable?", "o": ["Believe", "Teacher", "Police", "Machine"], "a": 1, "e": "'TEACHer' is stressed on the first syllable; the others on the second.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The rise and fall of the pitch of the voice when speaking is called:", "o": ["Stress", "Intonation", "Rhyme", "Rhythm"], "a": 1, "e": "Intonation is the variation in pitch across speech.", "d": 1},
    {"t": "The pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in poetry is called:", "o": ["Meter", "Rhyme", "Stanza", "Theme"], "a": 0, "e": "Meter is the rhythmic pattern of stressed/unstressed syllables.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Which of the following words is a homophone of 'write'?", "o": ["Ride", "Right", "Rite (both b and c)", "Rate"], "a": 2, "e": "'Write', 'right' and 'rite' all sound alike but differ in spelling/meaning.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "A word that has the same spelling but different meanings is a:", "o": ["Homophone", "Homograph", "Synonym", "Antonym"], "a": 1, "e": "A homograph is spelt the same but has different meanings (e.g. 'bank').", "d": 2},
    {"t": "'Sea' and 'see' are examples of:", "o": ["Synonyms", "Homophones", "Homographs", "Antonyms"], "a": 1, "e": "They sound alike but differ in spelling and meaning: homophones.", "d": 1},
    {"t": "In a declarative statement, the intonation pattern is typically:", "o": ["Rising", "Falling", "Level", "Rising-falling"], "a": 1, "e": "Statements normally end with falling intonation.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Yes/no questions typically end with ___ intonation.", "o": ["Falling", "Rising", "Level", "Monotone"], "a": 1, "e": "Yes/no questions use rising intonation at the end.", "d": 2},

    # ── Comprehension / vocabulary in context ──
    {"t": "In a passage, if an author describes a character as 'frugal', the character is:", "o": ["Wasteful", "Careful with money", "Dishonest", "Generous"], "a": 1, "e": "'Frugal' means economical and careful about spending.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The primary purpose of a persuasive passage is to:", "o": ["Narrate a story", "Describe a place", "Convince the reader", "List facts only"], "a": 2, "e": "Persuasive writing aims to convince or persuade the reader.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "In comprehension, the main idea of a passage is best found by:", "o": ["Reading only the first word", "Identifying the central point the writer makes", "Counting paragraphs", "Ignoring the title"], "a": 1, "e": "The main idea is the central point or message of the passage.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "A summary of a passage should:", "o": ["Be longer than the original", "Include the key points only", "Copy sentences word-for-word", "Add the reader's opinion"], "a": 1, "e": "A summary condenses a passage to its key points without copying.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'emigrate' means to:", "o": ["Enter a country", "Leave one's country to live elsewhere", "Travel for fun", "Return home"], "a": 1, "e": "'Emigrate' means to leave one's country to settle in another.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "'Immigrate' means to:", "o": ["Leave a country", "Enter and settle in a new country", "Visit briefly", "Become a citizen automatically"], "a": 1, "e": "'Immigrate' means to come into a country to live there.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correct option: 'The lecture was so boring that most students ___ off.'", "o": ["dozed", "dazed", "drowsed", "dozes"], "a": 0, "e": "'Dozed off' is the correct phrasal verb meaning to fall asleep briefly.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correct option: 'He was accused ___ stealing.'", "o": ["for", "of", "with", "about"], "a": 1, "e": "'Accused of' is the correct collocation.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correct option: 'She was absorbed ___ her book.'", "o": ["in", "at", "on", "by"], "a": 0, "e": "'Absorbed in' is the standard collocation.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correct option: 'The boys were divided ___ two groups.'", "o": ["into", "in", "on", "with"], "a": 0, "e": "'Divided into' is the correct collocation.", "d": 1},
    {"t": "Choose the correct option: 'I am fed up ___ this noise.'", "o": ["of", "with", "by", "for"], "a": 1, "e": "'Fed up with' is the standard expression.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correct option: 'He congratulated her ___ her success.'", "o": ["for", "on", "at", "with"], "a": 1, "e": "'Congratulate someone on something' is correct.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the correct option: 'The teacher insisted ___ punctuality.'", "o": ["for", "on", "at", "in"], "a": 1, "e": "'Insist on' is the correct collocation.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Select the correct option: 'We should always aim ___ excellence.'", "o": ["at", "for", "on", "to"], "a": 0, "e": "'Aim at' is the standard collocation.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the option nearest in meaning to 'candid' in: 'He gave a candid account of the event.'", "o": ["False", "Frank", "Brief", "Biased"], "a": 1, "e": "'Candid' here means frank and truthful.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "In the sentence 'The drought devastated the crops', 'devastated' means:", "o": ["Improved", "Destroyed", "Watered", "Harvested"], "a": 1, "e": "'Devastated' means ruined or destroyed completely.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "Choose the option nearest in meaning to the word in italics: 'His ARGUMENT was compelling.'", "o": ["Weak", "Convincing", "Long", "Confusing"], "a": 1, "e": "'Compelling' means convincing and persuasive.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "The word 'scanty' in 'scanty rainfall' means:", "o": ["Plentiful", "Meagre", "Heavy", "Regular"], "a": 1, "e": "'Scanty' means small or insufficient in amount; meagre.", "d": 2},
    {"t": "'The officer was impartial in his judgment.' 'Impartial' means:", "o": ["Biased", "Unfair", "Neutral", "Harsh"], "a": 2, "e": "'Impartial' means fair and unbiased; neutral.", "d": 2},
